[
    {
        "content": "<p>The Jacobian conjecture is a crazy relative of the inverse function theorem. </p>\n<p>The inverse function says that if <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>:</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">R</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>→</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">R</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F: \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}^n</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1389em;\">F</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">:</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6889em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathbb\">R</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.6644em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">n</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">→</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6889em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathbb\">R</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.6644em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">n</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span> is a smooth function and its matrix of partial deriatives <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∂</mi><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∂</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\partial F_i /\\partial x_j</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1.0361em;vertical-align:-0.2861em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\" style=\"margin-right:0.0556em;\">∂</span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1389em;\">F</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3117em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:-0.1389em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">i</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mord\">/</span><span class=\"mord\" style=\"margin-right:0.0556em;\">∂</span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">x</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3117em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:0em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\" style=\"margin-right:0.0572em;\">j</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.2861em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span> is invertible at some point, <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1389em;\">F</span></span></span></span> has a smooth inverse in some neighborhood of that point.</p>\n<p>The Jacobi Conjecture says that if <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>:</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">C</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>→</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">C</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F: \\mathbb{C}^n \\to \\mathbb{C}^n</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1389em;\">F</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">:</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6889em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathbb\">C</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.6644em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">n</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">→</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6889em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathbb\">C</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.6644em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">n</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span> is a polynomial function and its matrix of partial deriatives <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∂</mi><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∂</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\partial F_i /\\partial x_j</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1.0361em;vertical-align:-0.2861em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\" style=\"margin-right:0.0556em;\">∂</span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1389em;\">F</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3117em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:-0.1389em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">i</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mord\">/</span><span class=\"mord\" style=\"margin-right:0.0556em;\">∂</span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">x</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3117em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:0em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\" style=\"margin-right:0.0572em;\">j</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.2861em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span> is invertible at every point, <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1389em;\">F</span></span></span></span> has a polynomial inverse.</p>\n<p>This seems inherently implausible, but it was open since 1939, resisting attempts to find a counterexample... until yesterday.</p>",
        "id": 611615536,
        "sender_full_name": "John Baez",
        "timestamp": 1784536402
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>The mathematician Levent Alpöge, who works for Anthropic, found a counterexample to the Jacobi conjecture using Claude Fable.   It's easy enough to check:</p>\n<p>F(𝑥,𝑦,𝑧) = (𝑦²(3𝑥𝑦+4)(𝑥𝑦+1)+𝑧(𝑥𝑦+1)³, 3𝑥𝑦²(3𝑥𝑦+4)+3𝑥𝑧(𝑥𝑦+1)²+𝑦, 2𝑥−𝑥³𝑧−3𝑥²𝑦)</p>\n<p>has Jacobian determinant equal to -2 everywhere, but it's not invertible since </p>\n<p>F(0, 0, -1/4) = F(1, -3/2, 13/2) = F(-1, 3/2, 13/2) = (-1/4, 0, 0)</p>",
        "id": 611615893,
        "sender_full_name": "John Baez",
        "timestamp": 1784536535
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Omar Antolin passed on some tweets where Claude Fable kept rechecking its work, raising various questions, some quite hilarious.   </p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"Therefore the overwhelmingly likely resolution: THE MAP IS NOT ACTUALLY WHAT IT SEEMS - maybe it's not a map ℂ³→ℂ³?? It is.\" </p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><img alt=\"media_HNpQUaiWsAA43GQ.png\" class=\"inline-image\" data-original-content-type=\"image/png\" data-original-dimensions=\"1580x1216\" data-original-src=\"/user_uploads/21317/QHcTz9BkKpezToFmSbf8XRNB/media_HNpQUaiWsAA43GQ.png\" src=\"/user_uploads/thumbnail/21317/QHcTz9BkKpezToFmSbf8XRNB/media_HNpQUaiWsAA43GQ.png/840x560.webp\"></p>\n<p>Claude Fable, freaking out: </p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"... but come ON.  If this simple example were valid, [the Jacobi Conjecture] would have been settled long ago: this is clearly designed as a puzzle for AI/humans.\" </p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>But no: sometimes famous open conjectures have fairly simple solutions!</p>\n<p><img alt=\"media_HNpQXPsWoAAvXPH.png\" class=\"inline-image\" data-original-content-type=\"image/png\" data-original-dimensions=\"1580x1462\" data-original-src=\"/user_uploads/21317/FpFXD48ujQco_7gjb3txxOMy/media_HNpQXPsWoAAvXPH.png\" src=\"/user_uploads/thumbnail/21317/FpFXD48ujQco_7gjb3txxOMy/media_HNpQXPsWoAAvXPH.png/840x560.webp\"></p>",
        "id": 611616250,
        "sender_full_name": "John Baez",
        "timestamp": 1784536664
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Any idea already what the consequences are for the closely related Dixmier conjecture? There are some subtleties regarding determinant 1 vs. nonzero determinant and n vs. 2n.</p>",
        "id": 611617870,
        "sender_full_name": "Jens Hemelaer",
        "timestamp": 1784537247
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Ah yes, how could we have not spotted this trio of polynomials in 3 variables featuring at least one term of degree 7? Very careless in retrospect.</p>",
        "id": 611618153,
        "sender_full_name": "Morgan Rogers (he/him)",
        "timestamp": 1784537343
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"282722\">Jens Hemelaer</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/411257-theory.3A-mathematics/topic/Jacobi.20Conjecture.20disproved/near/611617870\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Any idea already what the consequences are for the closely related Dixmier conjecture? There are some subtleties regarding determinant 1 vs. nonzero determinant and n vs. 2n.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><a href=\"https://x.com/jrpickhardt/status/2079088546254925860\">Here</a>'s a claimed negative resolution built on this.</p>",
        "id": 611618909,
        "sender_full_name": "Martti Karvonen",
        "timestamp": 1784537599
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"275920\">John Baez</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/411257-theory.3A-mathematics/topic/Jacobi.20Conjecture.20disproved/near/611616250\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Claude Fable kept rechecking its work, raising various questions, some quite hilarious. </p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"Therefore the overwhelmingly likely resolution: THE MAP IS NOT ACTUALLY WHAT IT SEEMS - maybe it's not a map ℂ³→ℂ³?? It is.\" </p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><img alt=\"media_HNpQUaiWsAA43GQ.png\" class=\"inline-image\" data-original-content-type=\"image/png\" data-original-dimensions=\"1580x1216\" data-original-src=\"/user_uploads/21317/QHcTz9BkKpezToFmSbf8XRNB/media_HNpQUaiWsAA43GQ.png\" src=\"/user_uploads/thumbnail/21317/QHcTz9BkKpezToFmSbf8XRNB/media_HNpQUaiWsAA43GQ.png/840x560.webp\"></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>hmm hmm hmm... I wonder why the training set of Claude is skewed towards these affectations and not others.</p>",
        "id": 611622534,
        "sender_full_name": "fosco",
        "timestamp": 1784538836
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>If anything, this shows the sheer power of AI of getting rid of all the factoids that are a few brute-force attempts away from being answered. In a perfect world this would skew the power dynamics of mathematics towards structural thinking over computation... in our world, I don't know.</p>",
        "id": 611622784,
        "sender_full_name": "fosco",
        "timestamp": 1784538928
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>One thing: \"this seems inherently implausible\"</p>\n<p>does it?!</p>",
        "id": 611622934,
        "sender_full_name": "fosco",
        "timestamp": 1784538992
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I don't see anything saying \"this seems inherently implausible\".   But I  Fable is correct to worry</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>This example is suspiciously compact.  If a 3-to-1 Keller map on <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">C</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbb{C}^3</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8141em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathbb\">C</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8141em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">3</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span> this simple existed, it would have been found decades ago by computer searches.</p>\n</blockquote>",
        "id": 611643173,
        "sender_full_name": "John Baez",
        "timestamp": 1784546694
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><em>you</em> said “this seems inherently implausible” about the conjecture itself, John! I do wonder why Fable is wrong that this should have been found long ago by old-fashioned computer search.</p>",
        "id": 611643278,
        "sender_full_name": "Kevin Carlson",
        "timestamp": 1784546733
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Oh, okay.   I thought Fosco was talking about Fable's solution.    Yeah, the conjecture <em>itself</em> seems inherently implausible in a naive <em>prima facie</em> way, not based on any deep knowledge of algebraic geometry. </p>\n<p>Why should local invertibility imply global invertibility?   For example, everyone learns in school an analytic map <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>:</mo><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">C</mi><mo>→</mo><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f : \\mathbb{C} \\to \\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8889em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1076em;\">f</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">:</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6889em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathbb\">C</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">→</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6889em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathbb\">C</span></span></span></span> such that the determinant of its Jacobian is invertible everywhere, so <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8889em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1076em;\">f</span></span></span></span> is locally one-to-one, but <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8889em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1076em;\">f</span></span></span></span> fails to be one-to-one.   But the Jacobian conjecture says this can't happen for polynomials.  So somehow the magic would need to come from replacing \"analytic\" by \"polynomial\".</p>\n<p>Of course, a lot of magic comes from replacing \"analytic\" by \"polynomial\".   So maybe if I actually understood algebraic geometry, I'd know something that makes the Jacobian conjecture seem plausible.   But I don't, so it always seemed amazing-if-true.</p>",
        "id": 611644150,
        "sender_full_name": "John Baez",
        "timestamp": 1784547059
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Has anyone tried trying to resolve the Hadamard conjecture this way? I guess it's a slightly different type of conjecture but is something that strikes me as something likely to be in the convex hull of human knowledge (and, as of yesterday I would have imagined it to be infinitely easier than the Jacobian conjecture).</p>",
        "id": 611678253,
        "sender_full_name": "Oisín Flynn-Connolly",
        "timestamp": 1784557498
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I hadn't even known about the <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hadamard_matrix#Hadamard_conjecture\">Hadamard conjecture</a>:</p>\n<p>For every integer <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0315em;\">k</span></span></span></span> there's a <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><mi>k</mi><mo>×</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4k \\times 4k</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7778em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">4</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0315em;\">k</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">×</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">4</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0315em;\">k</span></span></span></span> matrix whose entries are either +1 or −1 and whose rows are mutually <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orthogonal_matrix\">orthogonal</a>.</p>\n<p>Wikipedia says that in 2014, there were 12 multiples of 4 less than 2000 for which no Hadamard matrix of that order was known.<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hadamard_matrix#cite_note-dokovic-9\">[9]</a> They are: 668, 716, 892, 1132, 1244, 1388, 1436, 1676, 1772, 1916, 1948, and 1964.</p>\n<p>So, the easiest thing for a LLM to try might be to find a Hadamard matrix of order  668.   Proving that none exists could be hard.  </p>\n<p>Unlike the Jacobian conjecture, where the counterexample was very easy to check, a counterexample to the Hadamard conjecture is a statement that at least <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>668</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>668</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2^{668 \\times 668}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8141em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\">2</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8141em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">668</span><span class=\"mbin mtight\">×</span><span class=\"mord mtight\">668</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span> matrices don't have rows that are mutually orthogonal!</p>",
        "id": 611679628,
        "sender_full_name": "John Baez",
        "timestamp": 1784557874
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>In principle, sure, but if it's false,  there's likely some kind of combinatorial obstruction that may already exist in some form the literature (or a general construction, but my guess is that it is probably false, else one would already have been found).</p>",
        "id": 611681626,
        "sender_full_name": "Oisín Flynn-Connolly",
        "timestamp": 1784558362
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>By the way: an \"old question of Grothendieck about whether every finite free group scheme of order n was killed by n\" was resolved in the negative on July 11th by ChatGPT Sol.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Kevin Buzzard, <a href=\"https://xenaproject.wordpress.com/2026/07/20/human-mathematicians-are-being-outcounterexampled/\">Human mathematicians are being counterexampled</a>, July 20th, 2026.</li>\n</ul>",
        "id": 611686805,
        "sender_full_name": "John Baez",
        "timestamp": 1784559625
